          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8.1.2 Types of mixtures"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.093,
            0.884,
            0.205
          ],
          "content": "You know that the term ‘mixture’ in common usage refers to the mixing of two or more components. The components of a mixture may themselves be mixtures, as in poha and sprout salad, or pure substances like sugar or common salt dissolved in water. However, in science, all the components of a mixture must be pure substances only."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.207,
            0.883,
            0.264
          ],
          "content": "Mixtures could be of several types depending on the physical state of their components. Some mixtures with their examples are shown in Table 8.1. Complete the third column—"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.078,
            0.297,
            0.874,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": "| S.No. | Mixture-type | Examples | Uniform or non-uniform |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. | Gas and gas | Air | Uniform |\n| 2. | Gas and liquid | Aerated water (soda water) Oxygen dissolved in water | …………………………………… |\n| 3. | Solid and gas | Carbon particles in air | ………………… |\n| 4. | Liquid and liquid | Acetic acid in water (vinegar) Oil and water | …………………………………… |\n| 5. | Solid and liquid | Sand and water Seawater | …………………………………… |\n| 6. | Solid and solid | Baking powder (baking soda and tartaric acid) Alloys | …………………………………… |",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.152,
                0.269,
                0.579,
                0.291
              ],
              "content": "~~Table 8.1: Different types of mixtu~~res"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_table_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.052,
            0.646,
            0.139,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.588,
            0.884,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": "You learnt in earlier grades about the separation of mixtures. It is done to separate the components of a mixture. The examples discussed were from everyday life, where separation is done to obtain the component of interest and other components are discarded. However, in science, the purpose of separating a mixture is to obtain pure substances."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.659,
            0.251,
            0.768
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
